  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warming and drying trend Friday through Sunday; cool/cold \n   valley lows with patchy frost.\n\n-  Westerly winds increase Friday afternoon and Monday with\n   passing disturbances.\n\nZonal flow aloft will yield to a building high-pressure ridge \nacross the Northern Rockies Friday through Saturday. This will \ninitiate a warming and drying trend, with daytime high \ntemperatures rising roughly 10 to 15 degrees (or more) above \nseasonal averages this weekend. Strong nocturnal radiational \ncooling under clear skies will allow valley temperatures to drop \ninto the 30s overnight, producing patchy frost in cold-prone \nlocations. Isolated, shallow morning valley fog is possible in \nlow-lying basins, though widespread impacts are unlikely. \n\nA weak upper-level shortwave tracking along the Canadian border \nFriday afternoon into Friday night will temporarily tighten \npressure gradients, increasing westerly surface wind gusts to 20 \nto 25 mph across Northwest Montana and higher terrain.\n\nUpper-level high pressure will maintain dry, warm conditions on\nSunday. A second weak disturbance will slide east through the\nregion Monday into Tuesday, flattening the ridge and bringing an \nuptick in westerly winds. Some destabilization associated with \nthis feature may support isolated afternoon showers and deeper\nmixing. High pressure will rebuild mid-to-late next week, \nmaintaining dry and warm conditions. Long-range ensemble guidance \nshows a low probability (40%) of an upper-level change knocking\ndown the ridge around October 9th to 10th, though confidence \nremains low this far out.",
